===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fro|resonance}} (French {{m|fr|résonance}}), from {{uder|en|la|resonantia||echo}}, from {{m|la|resonō||I resound}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛzənəns/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-resonance.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[quality#Noun|quality]] of being [[resonant]].
#: {{synonyms|en|resound}}
#* {{RQ:Stoker Dracula|passage=The leiter-wagons contained great, square boxes, with handles of thick rope; these were evidently empty by the ease with which the Slovaks handled them, and by their '''resonance''' as they were roughly moved.}}
# A resonant sound, [[echo]], or [[reverberation]], such as that produced by blowing over the top of a bottle.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|author=Edith Wharton|authorlink=Edith Wharton|title={{w|The Reef (novel)|The Reef}}|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/283/pg283-images.html|passage=He passed on, and the lights and cries of the station dropped away, merged in a wider haze and a hollower '''resonance''', as the train gathered itself up with a long shake and rolled out again into the darkness.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} The sound produced by a [[hollow]] body part such as the chest cavity upon [[auscultation]], especially that produced while the patient is speaking.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Something that [[evoke]]s an [[association]], or a strong [[emotion]].
#: {{coi|en|emotional '''resonance'''}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=May 24, 2012
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=Film: Reviews: Men In Black 3
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/men-in-black-3,75576/
|page=
|passage=But the film is largely redeemed by an unexpected emotional '''resonance''' befitting a Steven Spielberg production.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/postcolonial-blog/2017/oct/27/the-whole-recognition-process-has-a-deep-colonial-resonance|title=The whole recognition process has a deep colonial resonance|author=Paul Daley|date=2017-10-27|work=The Guardian|passage=The whole recognition process has a deep colonial '''resonance'''. [title]}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2022/nov/13/it-has-added-political-resonance-this-year-why-charles-dickenss-a-christmas-carol-still-strikes-a-chord|title=‘It has added political resonance this year’: why Charles Dickens’s A Christmas Carol still strikes a chord|date=2022-11-13|author=Vanessa Thorpe|work=The Guardian|passage=For audiences, all these shows mean a chance to revisit a story that still chimes loudly, and to see whether, as many suspect, it will have a more chilling '''resonance''' in the winter of 2022.}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} The [[increase]] in the [[amplitude]] of an [[oscillation]] of a [[system]] under the influence of a [[periodic]] [[force]] whose [[frequency]] is close to that of the system's [[natural]] frequency.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Charles P. Slichter|title=Principles of Magnetic Resonance|publisher=Springer Science & Business|isbn=9783662127841|page=217|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pWzrCAAAQBAJ&pg=PA217&dq=%22resonance%22&hl=&cd=6&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22resonance%22&f=false|text=One of the most important developments beyond the original concept of magnetic '''resonance''' is so-called double '''resonance''' in which, as the name suggests, one excites one resonant transition of a system while simultaneously monitoring a different transition.}}
# {{lb|en|nuclear physics}} A short-lived [[subatomic particle]] or state of [[atomic]] [[excitation]] that results from the [[collision]] of atomic [[particle]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|text=When experiments with the first ‘atom-smashers’ took place in the 1950s to 1960s, many short-lived heavier siblings of the proton and neutron, known as ‘'''resonances'''’, were discovered.|author=Frank Close|title=Particle Physics: A Very Short Introduction|location=Oxford|page=35}}
# An increase in the [[strength]] or [[duration]] of a [[musical]] [[tone]] produced by [[sympathetic vibration]].
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} The property of a [[compound]] that can be visualized as having two [[structure]]s differing only in the distribution of [[electron]]s.
#: {{syn|en|mesomerism}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} A influence of the [[gravitational]] [[force]]s of one [[orbit]]ing object on the orbit of another, causing [[periodic]] [[perturbation]]s.
# {{lb|en|electronics}} The condition where the [[inductive]] and [[capacitive]] [[reactance]]s have equal [[magnitude]].
# {{lb|en|sociology}} A quality of human relationship with the world.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|origyear=2016|author=James Wagner, transl.|by={{w|Hartmut Rosa}}|title=Resonance|original=Resonanz|isbn=9781509519927|url=https://books.google.com/books?hl=&id=nMOlDwAAQBAJ&dq=hartmut+rosa+resonance&q=resonance#v=snippet&q=resonance&f=false|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|passage='''Resonance''' is a kind of relationship to the world, formed through affect and emotion, intrinsic interest, and perceived self-efficacy, in which subject and world are mutually affected and transformed.}}

